  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n- More patchy areas of dense fog tonight\n\nHigh pressure remains over the Great Lakes tonight with calm winds \nand clear skies which means another night of good radiational \ncooling conditions. Similar to the past few nights, patch dense fog \nwill form in low spots, especially near rivers and inland lakes \nwhere air temperatures cool below the still relatively warm water \ntemperatures resulting in shallow, but dense \"steam fog\".\n\n- Rain Wednesday through Friday\n\nAn upper low across the western CONUS will move east and pull \nmoisture from the Eastern Pacific tropical cyclone with it, which \nspreads in a narrow ribbon along a nearly-east west frontal \nboundary by mid-week. We expect the rain to arrive by Wednesday \nafternoon and continue through Friday as the front moves slowly \nsoutheast, nearly parallel with the upper flow, with occasional \nbreaks in the steady rain. Total QPF from this event is expected \nto be 1 to 2 inches by Friday, but we will have to watch if it \noverperforms from the injection of tropical moisture.",
